Football Match Performance Tracker
===================================

A command-line tool for recording football match stats per player and
tracking how their performance changes over time.

Problem
-------
Professional analytics platforms are built for clubs with budgets to match;
fan-facing apps only show live scores. Small or amateur teams have no
lightweight way to track a player's form over a season. This tool fills
that gap: enter stats after each match, and it builds up a persistent
history you can summarize or graph at any time.

What it does
------------
- Keeps a roster of players and, for each, a log of per-match stats
  (goals, assists, saves, minutes played).
- Turns that log into totals, averages, and a single "performance score"
  per match, weighted however you choose (e.g. goals count for more than
  assists).
- Plots the performance score over time so form trends are visible at a
  glance, instead of buried in a table of numbers.
- Everything is saved between runs — nothing is lost when the program
  closes.

Architecture
------------
The program is split by responsibility rather than kept in one file:

- `database.py` owns persistence. It is the only module that talks to
  SQLite, and every other module reaches the data through it rather than
  running queries itself. This keeps storage swappable in principle and
  makes it obvious where to look for anything data-related.
- `calculations.py` is pure computation: given match data, it derives
  totals, averages, and weighted scores. It has no knowledge of the
  database or the terminal — it just transforms numbers.
- `visualization.py` turns computed scores into a chart. It depends on
  `calculations.py` but nothing depends on it, since plotting is an
  end point, not something other logic builds on.
- `main.py` is the interactive shell: it shows the menu, collects and
  validates input, and calls into the other three modules. It contains
  no business logic of its own — its job is coordination.

This separation means each layer can be reasoned about (and tested)
independently: the scoring formula doesn't care where the numbers came
from, and the storage layer doesn't care how the numbers will be shown.

Data model
----------
Three tables: players, matches (linked to a player, one row per match),
and a single settings-style row holding the current scoring weights.
Deleting a player removes their match history with them, so the data
never ends up in an inconsistent state.

Running it
----------
    cd /Users/raf/Documents/football
    source venv/bin/activate
    python3 main.py

The menu is numbered; type a number and press Enter. Data lives in
`football.db` in this folder and survives restarts.

Status
------
Core functionality is in place: add/edit/delete for both players and
matches, persistent storage, stat calculations, custom weights, and
trend plotting. Input is validated throughout (numbers, dates, ID
lookups) so bad input doesn't crash the program.
